  "findings": {
    "parent_meets_planning_utilization": "The parent's selected configuration runs at 26.9% benchmark MFU (25.7% at 6 x parameters), matching the plan's former 25% assumption within 3% after the overhead derate.",
    "rungs_fall_short_of_it": "Smaller models cannot fill the card: at their fastest measured microbatch, 410m, 130m and 50m run at 0.77, 0.54 and 0.43 of the rate a uniform 25% would give. Each rung's fastest microbatch was the largest the packet tried.",
    "microbatch_is_the_lever": "Microbatch one to four is 2.65x on the parent; compile alone is 1.036x, because chunk_kda and the Liger loss are compiler-disabled and cause all six graph breaks.",
    "loader_is_not_a_bottleneck": "End-to-end on the pilot pack is within the noise band of compute mode.",
    "determinism_cost": "Non-deterministic execution is 3.9% faster at the selected microbatch.",
    "liger_aligned": "11% faster on the parent across three repeats, forty times the noise band; single runs at the rungs are inside their larger step-time variation. It still fails restart parity.",
    "profile": "A cutlass_75 align1 GEMM, an unaligned fallback, takes 4.9% of kernel time on the parent. The 32,003-token vocabulary head is the likely source; liger_aligned pads it to a multiple of 64 and also accumulates in FP32. The profile summary files the H100 nvjet cuBLAS kernels under other, so its gemm share is understated.",
    "power_cap": "Clocks dipped below 95% of 1980 MHz in a few five-second samples in most runs, at the 700 W cap. Five repeats agree within 0.27%, so the dips do not move the rates.",
    "pilot_recipe_discrepancy": "The pilot recipe ran at 9,599 tokens/s in compute mode here, against the 18 September pilot's 13,595 steady optimizer tokens/s on another H100 host. Host, driver and code have all changed since; this sweep cannot attribute the gap. The eager microbatch-one recipe is launch-bound and the selected configuration is not (p90/median 1.001)."
  },
